<item>
  <title>Understanding Transition Probabilities</title>
  <dc:creator>Minkoo Kang</dc:creator>
  <link>https://mkw0rld.github.io/posts/Test/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="definition" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="definition">Definition</h2>
<p>Let ({X_n}_{n}) be a Markov chain.</p>
<p>The transition probability from state (i) to state (j) is defined as</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Ap(i,j)%0A=%0AP(X_%7Bn+1%7D=j%20%5Cmid%20X_n=i).%0A"></p>
</section>
<section id="interpretation" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="interpretation">Interpretation</h2>
<p>The quantity (p(i,j)) represents the probability that the chain moves to state (j) at the next step, given that its current state is (i).</p>
</section>
<section id="two-step-transition-probabilities" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="two-step-transition-probabilities">Two-step transition probabilities</h2>
<p>To move from (i) to (j) in two steps, the chain may pass through any intermediate state (k). Therefore,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Ap%5E%7B(2)%7D(i,j)%0A=%0A%5Csum_%7Bk%5Cin%20S%7Dp(i,k)p(k,j).%0A"></p>


</section>

 ]]></description>
